+"""
+    compact_reduction_derivative_applicable(D, a, b)
+
+Whether it is safe to use the compact `dot`/`norm` derivative rules for `D.x` at all.
+It is NOT safe when `D.x` is itself a scalar index into `a` or `b` -- e.g.
+`Symbolics.derivative(dot(x, x), x[1])`, an existing, tested Symbolics idiom (differentiate
+an array reduction with respect to one scalar component of the *same* array). There, the
+correct derivative genuinely depends on picking out just that one component (all other
+components contribute zero), which is exactly what full scalarization computes; `D(x)`
+left compact and unexpanded would be wrong (it does not collapse to the standard basis
+vector `[1,0,0]` the way plain scalarized per-component differentiation naturally does).
+This is the mirror image of `compact_dot_derivative`'s `D.x` being a functional/time-like
+variable that `a`/`b` merely *depend on* (e.g. `p(t)` differentiated wrt `t`), which is the
+case the compact rule is for and where it is correct.
+"""
+function compact_reduction_derivative_applicable(D::Differential, a::SymbolicT, b::SymbolicT)
+    is_scalar_indexed(D.x) || return true
+    base = arguments(D.x)[1]
+    return !isequal(base, a) && !isequal(base, b)
+end
+
+"""
+    compact_dot_derivative(D, a, b)
+
+Compact (non-scalarizing) product-rule derivative of `LinearAlgebra.dot(a, b)` for
+array-shaped symbolic `a`, `b`: `D(dot(a,b)) = dot(D(a), b) + dot(a, D(b))`. Caller must
+first check `compact_reduction_derivative_applicable(D, a, b)`.
+
+`a`/`b` are array-shaped, so we cannot recurse via `executediff` (it does not support
+differentiating a bare array expression directly: `occursin_info` with its default
+`fail=true` throws "Differentiation with array expressions is not yet supported" on
+array-shaped input). Instead we build `D(a)`/`D(b)` directly via the `Differential`
+callable (which just wraps the argument in an unexpanded `Differential` application,
+exactly like the ordinary scalar case `D(y) -> Differential(x,1)(y)`), and use
+`occursin_info(D.x, ·, false)` to check occurrence -- `fail=false` selects the safe,
+shape-agnostic `SymbolicUtils.query` search, exactly as `_occursin_info` already does
+internally when it recurses into a `dot`/`norm` call's arguments (see the `cond`
+computation a few dozen lines above, in `_occursin_info`).
+"""
+function compact_dot_derivative(D::Differential, a::SymbolicT, b::SymbolicT)
+    a_occurs = occursin_info(D.x, a, false)
+    b_occurs = occursin_info(D.x, b, false)
+    if !a_occurs && !b_occurs
+        return COMMON_ZERO
+    elseif isequal(a, b)
+        # D(dot(a,a)) = 2*dot(a, D(a))
+        return 2 * LinearAlgebra.dot(a, D(a))
+    elseif !a_occurs
+        return LinearAlgebra.dot(a, D(b))
+    elseif !b_occurs
+        return LinearAlgebra.dot(D(a), b)
+    else
+        return LinearAlgebra.dot(D(a), b) + LinearAlgebra.dot(a, D(b))
+    end
+end
+
+"""
+    compact_norm_derivative(D, a)
+
+Compact (non-scalarizing) chain-rule derivative of `LinearAlgebra.norm(a)`. This matches
+the *existing* scalarized branch's semantics exactly: that branch computes
+`sqrt(sum(abs2(a[i])))`, which is precisely the 2-norm and precisely equal to
+`sqrt(LinearAlgebra.dot(a, a))` (for both real and complex `a`, since `dot` conjugates
+its first argument). So `D(norm(a)) = dot(a, D(a)) / norm(a)` reproduces the same
+2-norm semantics compactly. See `compact_dot_derivative` for why `occursin_info(D.x, a,
+false)` rather than `executediff` is used to test occurrence.
+"""
+function compact_norm_derivative(D::Differential, a::SymbolicT)
+    occursin_info(D.x, a, false) || return COMMON_ZERO
+    return LinearAlgebra.dot(a, D(a)) / LinearAlgebra.norm(a)
+end
